# Monitor Jitter and Fidelity Priority

The monitor runs on overloaded systems and must minimize interference with user workloads.

## Core requirements

- Treat CPU jitter and monitor cycle consumption as first-class constraints for all monitor changes.
- Prefer bounded, deterministic, and amortized work in hot paths (`sample_timer`, `send_timer`, per-collector `st_collect`).
- Avoid unbounded loops and repeated control-plane setup/teardown inside recurring callbacks.
- Use caches, handle reuse, cooldowns, and backoff to prevent repeated expensive probes/retries.

## Sampling fidelity policy

- Do not increase sampling interval as the default fix for CPU pressure.
- Preserve data fidelity at existing cadence; optimize work per sample instead of reducing sample count.
- If a cadence change is ever proposed, require explicit user approval and document the data-quality tradeoff.

## Implementation guidance

- Move heavyweight discovery/init to startup, explicit reload paths, or failure recovery paths.
- Keep recurring collect paths focused on lightweight reads and bounded processing.
- Add low-noise diagnostics that prove progress and identify stalls without creating additional jitter.

